-artificial-intelligence-bias">What is Artificial Intelligence Bias</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/who-is-the-father-of-artificial-intelligence">Who is the Father of Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sop-for-ms-in-artificial-intelligence">SOP for MS in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/top-ai-penny-stocks-in-india">Top AI Penny Stocks in India</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-ai-in-data-analytics">What is AI in Data Analytics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-business-management">Artificial Intelligence in Business Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/partial-order-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Partial Order Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-auditing">Artificial Intelligence in Auditing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-construction">Artificial Intelligence in Construction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/information-retrieval-in-artificial-intelligence">Information Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-psychology">Artificial Intelligence in Psychology</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iterative-deepening-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Iterative Deepening Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/applications-of-deep-learning-in-artificial-intelligence">Applications of Deep Learning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/7-artificial-intelligence-projects-in-agriculture">7 Artificial Intelligence Projects in Agriculture</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-multimodal-ai">What is Multimodal AI</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/benefits-of-artificial-intelligence-in-business">Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in Business</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conceptual-graphs-in-artificial-intelligence">Conceptual Graphs in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/problem-space-and-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Problem Space and Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Conditional Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/informed-search-algorithms-in-artificial-intelligence">Informed Search Algorithms in Artificial Intelligence</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="ai-alpha-beta-pruning">next →</a> <a class="next" href="ai-adversarial-search">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Mini-Max Algorithm in Artificial Intelligence</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>17 Mar 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 10 min read</span></div><ul class="points"><li>Mini-max algorithm is a recursive or backtracking algorithm which is used in decision-making and game theory. It provides an optimal move for the player assuming that opponent is also playing optimally.</li><li>Mini-Max algorithm uses recursion to search through the game-tree.</li><li>Min-Max algorithm is mostly used for game playing in AI. Such as Chess, Checkers, tic-tac-toe, go, and various tow-players game. This Algorithm computes the minimax decision for the current state.</li><li>In this algorithm two players play the game, one is called MAX and other is called MIN.</li><li>Both the players fight it as the opponent player gets the minimum benefit while they get the maximum benefit.</li><li>Both Players of the game are opponent of each other, where MAX will select the maximized value and MIN will select the minimized value.</li><li>The minimax algorithm performs a depth-first search algorithm for the exploration of the complete game tree.</li><li>The minimax algorithm proceeds all the way down to the terminal node of the tree, then backtrack the tree as the recursion.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Pseudo-code for MinMax Algorithm:</h2><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">function minimax(node, depth, maximizingPlayer) is
if depth ==0 or node is a terminal node then
return static evaluation of node

if MaximizingPlayer then      // for Maximizer Player
maxEva= -infinity          
 for each child of node do
 eva= minimax(child, depth-1, false)
maxEva= max(maxEva,eva)        //gives Maximum of the values
return maxEva

else                         // for Minimizer player
 minEva= +infinity 
 for each child of node do
 eva= minimax(child, depth-1, true)
 minEva= min(minEva, eva)         //gives minimum of the values
 return minEva
</textarea></div><p><strong>Initial call:</strong></p><p><strong>Minimax(node, 3, true)</strong></p><h2 class="h2">Working of Min-Max Algorithm:</h2><ul class="points"><li>The working of the minimax algorithm can be easily described using an example. Below we have taken an example of game-tree which is representing the two-player game.</li><li>In this example, there are two players one is called Maximizer and other is called Minimizer.</li><li>Maximizer will try to get the Maximum possible score, and Minimizer will try to get the minimum possible score.</li><li>This algorithm applies DFS, so in this game-tree, we have to go all the way through the leaves to reach the terminal nodes.</li><li>At the terminal node, the terminal values are given so we will compare those value and backtrack the tree until the initial state occurs. Following are the main steps involved in solving the two-player game tree:</li></ul><p><strong>Step-1:</strong> In the first step, the algorithm generates the entire game-tree and apply the utility function to get the utility values for the terminal states. In the below tree diagram, let's take A is the initial state of the tree. Suppose maximizer takes first turn which has worst-case initial value =- infinity, and minimizer will take next turn which has worst-case initial value = +infinity.</p><img alt="Mini-Max Algorithm in AI" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/ai/images/mini-max-algorithm-in-ai-step1.png"><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Now, first we find the utilities value for the Maximizer, its initial value is -∞, so we will compare each value in terminal state with initial value of Maximizer and determines the higher nodes values. It will find the maximum among the all.</p><ul class="points"><li>For node D         max(-1,- -∞) =&gt; max(-1,4)= 4</li><li>For Node E         max(2, -∞) =&gt; max(2, 6)= 6</li><li>For Node F         max(-3, -∞) =&gt; max(-3,-5) = -3</li><li>For node G         max(0, -∞) = max(0, 7) = 7</li></ul><img alt="Mini-Max Algorithm in AI" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/ai/images/mini-max-algorithm-in-ai-step2.png"><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In the next step, it's a turn for minimizer, so it will compare all nodes value with +∞, and will find the 3<sup>rd</sup> layer node values.</p><ul class="points"><li>For node B= min(4,6) = 4</li><li>For node C= min (-3, 7) = -3</li></ul><img alt="Mini-Max Algorithm in AI" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/ai/images/mini-max-algorithm-in-ai-step3.png"><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Now it's a turn for Maximizer, and it will again choose the maximum of all nodes value and find the maximum value for the root node. In this game tree, there are only 4 layers, hence we reach immediately to the root node, but in real games, there will be more than 4 layers.</p><ul class="points"><li>For node A max(4, -3)= 4</li></ul><img alt="Mini-Max Algorithm in AI" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/ai/images/mini-max-algorithm-in-ai-step4.png"><p>That was the complete workflow of the minimax two player game.</p><h2 class="h2">Properties of Mini-Max algorithm:</h2><ul class="points"><li><strong>Complete-</strong> Min-Max algorithm is Complete. It will definitely find a solution (if exist), in the finite search tree.</li><li><strong>Optimal-</strong> Min-Max algorithm is optimal if both opponents are playing optimally.</li><li><strong>Time complexity-</strong> As it performs DFS for the game-tree, so the time complexity of Min-Max algorithm is <strong>O(b<sup>m</sup>)</strong>, where b is branching factor of the game-tree, and m is the maximum depth of the tree.</li><li><strong>Space Complexity-</strong> Space complexity of Mini-max algorithm is also similar to DFS which is <strong>O(bm)</strong>.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Limitation of the minimax Algorithm:</h2><p>The main drawback of the minimax algorithm is that it gets really slow for complex games such as Chess, go, etc. This type of games has a huge branching factor, and the player has lots of choices to decide. This limitation of the minimax algorithm can be improved from <strong>alpha-beta pruning</strong> which we have discussed in the next topic.</p><hr/><h2 class="h2">MCQ's on the Mini-Max algorithm in Artificial Intelligence</h2><p class="pq"><strong>1. Which of the following best describes the Mini-Max algorithm in Artificial Intelligence?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>A heuristic-based search algorithm</li><li>A backtracking algorithm used for decision-making and game theory</li><li>A sorting algorithm</li><li>A reinforcement learning approach</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(1)"><span id="btntext1">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(1)"><span id="btntext1">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace1"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer1"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm is a recursive, backtracking algorithm used in decision-making and game theory. It helps determine the optimal move for a player assuming that the opponent also plays optimally.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>2. What is the primary purpose of the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>To find the shortest path in a graph</li><li>To classify datasets in machine learning</li><li>To make optimal moves in a two-player game</li><li>To predict the future state of a system</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(2)"><span id="btntext2">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(2)"><span id="btntext2">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace2"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer2"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm is primarily used in two-player games such as chess, tic-tac-toe, and checkers, where it helps in making optimal moves based on the assumption that the opponent also plays optimally.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>3. Which of the following best describes the working mechanism of the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It performs a breadth-first search</li><li>It applies a heuristic function to predict outcomes</li><li>It performs a depth-first search to explore the complete game tree</li><li>It relies on reinforcement learning techniques</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(3)"><span id="btntext3">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(3)"><span id="btntext3">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace3"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer3"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm performs a depth-first search (DFS) to explore all possible moves in a game-tree and determine the best possible move.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>4. In the Mini-Max algorithm, what is the role of the MAX player?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>To minimize the score of the game</li><li>To maximize the possible score in the game</li><li>To make random moves</li><li>To determine the depth of the game-tree</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(4)"><span id="btntext4">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(4)"><span id="btntext4">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace4"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer4"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The MAX player aims to maximize the game score by selecting the move with the highest possible value at each decision point.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>5. Which of the following is NOT a game where the Mini-Max algorithm is commonly used?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Chess</li><li>Tic-Tac-Toe</li><li>Checkers</li><li>Sudoku</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(5)"><span id="btntext5">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(5)"><span id="btntext5">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace5"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer5"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Mini-Max is used in two-player strategy games like Chess, Checkers, and Tic-Tac-Toe, but Sudoku is a single-player puzzle game and does not require the Mini-Max approach.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>6. What is the time complexity of the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>O (n log n)</li><li>O (bm)</li><li>O (mb)</li><li>O (2^n)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(6)"><span id="btntext6">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(6)"><span id="btntext6">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace6"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer6"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The time complexity of the Mini-Max algorithm is O(b^m), where 'b' is the branching factor of the game tree and 'm' is the maximum depth.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>7. What happens at the terminal nodes of a game tree in the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>The algorithm assigns heuristic values based on game outcomes</li><li>The game restarts with a new initial state</li><li>The players swap their roles</li><li>The algorithm selects a random move</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(7)"><span id="btntext7">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(7)"><span id="btntext7">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace7"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer7"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> At terminal nodes, the Mini-Max algorithm assigns heuristic values based on the game's final outcome, which are then propagated backward to determine the best move.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>8. Which strategy is used by the Mini-Max algorithm to traverse the game tree?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Breadth-first search</li><li>Depth-first search</li><li>A* search</li><li>Greedy search</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(8)"><span id="btntext8">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(8)"><span id="btntext8">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace8"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer8"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm employs depth-first search (DFS) to evaluate all possible game moves and determine the best decision.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>9. What is a major limitation of the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It cannot be used in two-player games</li><li>It does not guarantee an optimal solution</li><li>It becomes slow for games with a large branching factor</li><li>It does not use recursion</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(9)"><span id="btntext9">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(9)"><span id="btntext9">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace9"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer9"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> A major limitation of the Mini-Max algorithm is its inefficiency in games with a large branching factor, such as Chess and Go, where the number of possible moves is extremely high.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>10. Which optimization technique can be used to improve the efficiency of the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Reinforcement Learning</li><li>Alpha-Beta Pruning</li><li>Backpropagation</li><li>Genetic Algorithm</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(10)"><span id="btntext10">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(10)"><span id="btntext10">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace10"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer10"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Alpha-Beta Pruning is an optimization technique that helps improve the efficiency of the Mini-Max algorithm by eliminating branches that do not affect the final decision.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>11. Which condition causes the Mini-Max algorithm to terminate recursion?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>When a winning move is found</li><li>When the MAX player has a higher score than the MIN player</li><li>When all possible moves are evaluated</li><li>When the depth reaches zero or a terminal node is encountered</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(11)"><span id="btntext11">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(11)"><span id="btntext11">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace11"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer11"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm terminates when it reaches a predefined depth limit or a terminal node, where no further moves can be made.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>12. What is the main advantage of using Mini-Max over a purely heuristic-based algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It can handle incomplete information in games</li><li>It guarantees an optimal solution when the opponent plays optimally</li><li>It is computationally faster than other algorithms</li><li>It does not require an evaluation function</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(12)"><span id="btntext12">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(12)"><span id="btntext12">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace12"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer12"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Mini-Max ensures an optimal solution in deterministic, perfect-information games if the opponent also plays optimally.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>13. How does the Mini-Max algorithm evaluate intermediate nodes in the game tree?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>By applying reinforcement learning techniques</li><li>By computing the average of child node values</li><li>By propagating the minimax decision upwards from terminal nodes</li><li>By using a random number generator</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(13)"><span id="btntext13">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(13)"><span id="btntext13">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace13"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer13"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The Mini-Max algorithm recursively evaluates terminal nodes and propagates the best possible decision upwards through the game tree.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>14. What is the significance of the branching factor (b) in the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It represents the number of players in the game</li><li>It determines the maximum number of moves possible at each node</li><li>It controls the depth of the search tree</li><li>It is used to balance the heuristic evaluation function</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(14)"><span id="btntext14">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(14)"><span id="btntext14">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace14"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer14"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The branching factor (b) represents the number of possible moves at each decision point in the game tree.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>15. Which of the following is true about the Mini-Max algorithm's space complexity?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It is always constant regardless of game depth</li><li>It depends on the depth and branching factor of the game tree</li><li>It grows logarithmically with the game tree size</li><li>It is independent of the search depth</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(15)"><span id="btntext15">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(15)"><span id="btntext15">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace15"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer15"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Mini-Max uses depth-first search (DFS), and its space complexity is O(bm), where b is the branching factor and m is the depth of the tree.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>16. In which scenario is the Mini-Max algorithm least effective?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>A game with a small number of possible moves</li><li>A game with a large branching factor and deep game trees</li><li>A deterministic two-player game</li><li>A game where all possible moves have equal weight</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(16)"><span id="btntext16">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(16)"><span id="btntext16">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace16"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer16"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Mini-Max struggles with games that have a large branching factor and deep game trees, making computation expensive.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>17. What assumption does the Mini-Max algorithm make about the opponent's strategy?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>The opponent plays randomly</li><li>The opponent always plays optimally</li><li>The opponent follows a predefined set of moves</li><li>The opponent uses probabilistic decision-making</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(17)"><span id="btntext17">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(17)"><span id="btntext17">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace17"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer17"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Mini-Max assumes that the opponent will always make the best possible move to minimize the MAX player's advantage.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>18. What is the primary function of the evaluation function in the Mini-Max algorithm?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>To generate new moves</li><li>To determine the best possible move based on game heuristics</li><li>To randomly assign scores to game states</li><li>To calculate the probability of winning</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(18)"><span id="btntext18">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(18)"><span id="btntext18">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace18"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer18"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The evaluation function assesses the quality of game states and helps determine the best possible move when full tree traversal is infeasible.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>19. Why does the Mini-Max algorithm perform a full tree traversal in smaller games like Tic-Tac-Toe?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Because the branching factor is too small to cause computational overhead</li><li>Because it is necessary to analyze every possible move</li><li>Because it does not use heuristics</li><li>Because Tic-Tac-Toe is a one-player game</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(19)"><span id="btntext19">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(19)"><span id="btntext19">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace19"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer19"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Small games like Tic-Tac-Toe have a limited game tree, allowing Mini-Max to analyze all possible moves without performance issues.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>20. Which modification improves the efficiency of the Mini-Max algorithm by pruning unnecessary branches?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Breadth-First Search</li><li>Alpha-Beta Pruning</li><li>Greedy Search</li><li>Monte Carlo Tree Search</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(20)"><span id="btntext20">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(20)"><span id="btntext20">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace20"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer20"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Alpha-Beta pruning eliminates branches that do not affect the final decision, making the Mini-Max algorithm more efficient.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>21. What happens if an evaluation function is not used in the Mini-Max algorithm for deep games?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>The algorithm runs in constant time</li><li>The search space becomes too large to handle efficiently</li><li>The algorithm always finds the best move</li><li>The algorithm does not work for two-player games</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(21)"><span id="btntext21">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(21)"><span id="btntext21">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace21"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer21"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Without an evaluation function, Mini-Max would have to traverse the entire game tree, leading to excessive computation in deep games.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>22. How does the Mini-Max algorithm deal with uncertainty in games with incomplete information?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>By using probability distributions</li><li>By relying on the opponent's previous moves</li><li>By assuming the best possible move from the opponent</li><li>By applying reinforcement learning techniques</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(22)"><span id="btntext22">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(22)"><span id="btntext22">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace22"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer22"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> In games with incomplete information, Mini-Max can incorporate probability distributions to estimate unknown factors.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>23. Why is Mini-Max less commonly used in games like Poker or Blackjack?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>Because these games do not have opponents</li><li>Because these games involve hidden information and randomness</li><li>Because Mini-Max is only designed for single-player games</li><li>Because these games are too simple for Mini-Max to be useful</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(23)"><span id="btntext23">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(23)"><span id="btntext23">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace23"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer23"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Games like Poker and Blackjack involve hidden information and probabilistic outcomes, making Mini-Max less effective.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>24. How does the Mini-Max algorithm evaluate intermediate nodes when reaching maximum depth?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>By selecting a random score</li><li>By using a heuristic evaluation function</li><li>By ignoring the intermediate nodes</li><li>By assuming they are terminal nodes</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(24)"><span id="btntext24">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(24)"><span id="btntext24">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace24"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer24"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> When the search reaches maximum depth, Mini-Max applies a heuristic function to estimate the value of the intermediate nodes.</p></div><hr/><p class="pq"><strong>25. What effect does increasing the depth limit have on the Mini-Max algorithm’s performance?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>It improves decision-making without increasing computation</li><li>It exponentially increases computation time</li><li>It reduces the accuracy of the algorithm</li><li>It has no impact on algorithm performance</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(25)"><span id="btntext25">Show Answer</span></button> <button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(25)"><span id="btntext25">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace25"></div><div class="testanswer" id="answer25"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Increasing depth increases the number of possible moves to evaluate exponentially, leading to a rapid increase in computation time.</p></div><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="ai-alpha-beta-pruning">Alpha-Beta Pruning</a></span></div> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="ai-adversarial-search" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="ai-alpha-beta-pruning" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br> </br></img></img></img></img></td></tr></tbody></table></div><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="ai-alpha-beta-pruning"><h3 class="h3">Alpha-Beta Pruning</h3><p class="related-post-content">Alpha-beta pruning is a modified version of the minimax algorithm. It is an optimisation technique for the minimax algorithm. As we have seen in the minimax search algorithm, the number of game states it must examine is exponential in the depth of the tree. Since we...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 9 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-3.4-1.3-6.6